WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.332 --> 00:00:05.292
- Hello, and welcome to
Ed Talks with Khan Academy

00:00:05.292 --> 00:00:07.532
where we talk to influential people

00:00:07.532 --> 00:00:09.342
in the field of education.

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:12.459
I am excited today to talk
with Dr. Dan Willingham.

00:00:12.459 --> 00:00:14.802
Before we get started with that

00:00:14.802 --> 00:00:18.782
I want to remind all of
you that Khan Academy

00:00:18.782 --> 00:00:20.512
is a non-profit that relies

00:00:20.512 --> 00:00:23.312
on donations from folks like yourself to

00:00:23.312 --> 00:00:26.582
help us help keep more people learning

00:00:26.582 --> 00:00:31.582
and you can find the space
to donate there on our site.

00:00:32.452 --> 00:00:34.952
Also during the pandemic

00:00:34.952 --> 00:00:39.282
we have required some extra
supports to help keep us going

00:00:39.282 --> 00:00:40.778
and make sure

00:00:40.778 --> 00:00:42.772
that all learners have
those opportunities that

00:00:42.772 --> 00:00:46.572
they've needed for digital
learning in this time.

00:00:46.572 --> 00:00:49.297
And we want to particularly
thank Bank of America,

00:00:49.297 --> 00:00:54.297
AT&amp;T, Google.org, Novartis,
Fastly, and General Motors

00:00:55.322 --> 00:00:56.355
for their support.

00:00:59.032 --> 00:01:01.225
Thankful for all of
that, all of that help.

00:01:02.392 --> 00:01:06.142
And finally we want to
remind you that there's

00:01:06.142 --> 00:01:10.652
an audio version of our
ED Talks that's available.

00:01:10.652 --> 00:01:14.292
Homeroom With Sal, the
podcast, available wherever

00:01:14.292 --> 00:01:15.792
you get your podcasts.

00:01:15.792 --> 00:01:17.672
So, take a look at that.

00:01:17.672 --> 00:01:21.112
You can find previous
episodes and review favorites

00:01:21.112 --> 00:01:22.895
while you're looking for those.

00:01:23.742 --> 00:01:27.275
So today as I said, I'm excited to talk to

00:01:27.275 --> 00:01:30.622
Dr. Dan Willingham.

00:01:30.622 --> 00:01:34.272
When people ask me, hey
where can I read more

00:01:34.272 --> 00:01:37.502
about learning science
and how people learn,

00:01:37.502 --> 00:01:40.292
his book, "Why Don't Students Like School"

00:01:40.292 --> 00:01:43.782
is one of the first
places I send people to.

00:01:43.782 --> 00:01:47.712
And the second edition of
that book is out this week.

00:01:47.712 --> 00:01:51.042
So go find that at your
favorite book seller.

00:01:51.042 --> 00:01:54.652
And in addition to all
the previous content

00:01:54.652 --> 00:01:56.492
it also covers educational technology,

00:01:56.492 --> 00:01:59.752
which of course is a place
that is near and dear

00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:00.585
to my heart.

00:02:00.585 --> 00:02:03.655
But Dan, welcome and
thanks for joining us.

00:02:03.655 --> 00:02:05.742
- Hi Kristen, I'm so glad to be here,

00:02:05.742 --> 00:02:06.992
and thanks for having me.

00:02:08.202 --> 00:02:09.702
- Absolutely.

00:02:09.702 --> 00:02:12.222
So, I want to as usual in these talks,

00:02:12.222 --> 00:02:15.195
start off with some
questions that I have from

00:02:15.195 --> 00:02:17.852
reading your works, and then we'll go

00:02:17.852 --> 00:02:18.912
to audience questions.

00:02:18.912 --> 00:02:19.952
So those of you who are listening

00:02:19.952 --> 00:02:22.515
feel free to add things and questions into

00:02:22.515 --> 00:02:25.912
the chat and I will pick those up as well.

00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:27.892
But I'm interested in
starting and thinking

00:02:27.892 --> 00:02:31.642
about curiosity, which is
something that you talk

00:02:31.642 --> 00:02:35.315
about upfront in one
of your first chapters,

00:02:35.315 --> 00:02:40.062
and thinking about there's
things that it seems

00:02:40.062 --> 00:02:42.132
like we're curious about
and there's some natural

00:02:42.132 --> 00:02:43.962
curiosity when you think about young kids

00:02:43.962 --> 00:02:46.542
asking questions and where that is,

00:02:46.542 --> 00:02:49.422
but then sometimes it
seems to kind of fade away

00:02:49.422 --> 00:02:53.662
and there's like, we kind
of lose some of that.

00:02:53.662 --> 00:02:55.962
So, how should we think about curiosity in

00:02:55.962 --> 00:02:57.085
helping us learn?

00:02:58.052 --> 00:03:00.452
- So, yeah curiosity is one of the things

00:03:00.452 --> 00:03:03.002
that I just find so fascinating because

00:03:03.002 --> 00:03:05.082
I notice this in myself

00:03:05.082 --> 00:03:07.591
before I was a cognitive psychologist

00:03:07.591 --> 00:03:11.250
or studied any of these things formally.

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:16.250
We think of curiosity of
being driven by content.

00:03:16.412 --> 00:03:19.232
So, you think like, I'm
a cognitive psychologist,

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:22.202
so I'm curious about cognitive psychology.

00:03:22.202 --> 00:03:25.542
And the truth is like that's sort of true,

00:03:25.542 --> 00:03:29.852
but not remotely true as a general rule.

00:03:29.852 --> 00:03:33.292
I have been to many many
conferences and attended

00:03:33.292 --> 00:03:34.772
cognitive psychology talks

00:03:34.772 --> 00:03:37.222
and found myself extraordinarily bored.

00:03:37.222 --> 00:03:39.982
And I'm sure everybody
listening to this talk now

00:03:39.982 --> 00:03:41.312
has had the same experience.

00:03:41.312 --> 00:03:44.042
You go into something,
you start reading a book,

00:03:44.042 --> 00:03:45.622
or you watch a YouTube
video 'cause you're like,

00:03:45.622 --> 00:03:46.455
oh I love this topic.

00:03:46.455 --> 00:03:47.522
This is gonna be great.

00:03:48.388 --> 00:03:49.221
And then it's horrible.

00:03:49.221 --> 00:03:52.192
And then the opposite can
equally happen equally often

00:03:52.192 --> 00:03:56.602
where somebody sort of
forces you to watch a YouTube

00:03:56.602 --> 00:03:58.872
video about how they make
dumbbells and you're like,

00:03:58.872 --> 00:04:00.532
that can't be interesting.

00:04:00.532 --> 00:04:02.312
And then oh my god, it's
totally fascinating.

00:04:02.312 --> 00:04:05.452
So I think the through line for curiosity,

00:04:05.452 --> 00:04:08.162
you mentioned kids and how
kids seem to be curious

00:04:08.162 --> 00:04:12.102
about everything, and then
like we adults not so much.

00:04:12.102 --> 00:04:13.572
We're much more selective.

00:04:13.572 --> 00:04:15.802
The kind of through
line for that I think is

00:04:15.802 --> 00:04:20.392
curiosity is all about learning
about your environment.

00:04:20.392 --> 00:04:25.392
And so we are continually
sizing up our environment and

00:04:25.592 --> 00:04:29.882
figuring out is this,
is there an opportunity

00:04:29.882 --> 00:04:32.002
for learning here?

00:04:32.002 --> 00:04:37.002
And learning the way you
make that calculation is

00:04:37.856 --> 00:04:42.354
the unknown needs to be at
sort of just the right level.

00:04:42.354 --> 00:04:46.382
So, if you offer me some information,

00:04:46.382 --> 00:04:48.858
and it's a topic, like if you said to me,

00:04:48.858 --> 00:04:50.122
"Hey, would you like to
know who the first president

00:04:50.122 --> 00:04:51.718
of the U.S. is?"

00:04:51.718 --> 00:04:52.712
I'm like, that doesn't
make be very curious,

00:04:52.712 --> 00:04:54.540
because I already have
that information, right.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:57.612
Likewise, if you said, would
you like to know who the first

00:04:57.612 --> 00:05:01.912
president of Chad was, I'm like sort of.

00:05:01.912 --> 00:05:03.982
But I really don't know
anything about Chad,

00:05:03.982 --> 00:05:06.192
and so that's not gonna help me very much.

00:05:06.192 --> 00:05:10.572
It's when there's
information that's gonna add

00:05:10.572 --> 00:05:13.532
to what we already know,
that seems to be a really

00:05:13.532 --> 00:05:17.142
critical trigger for curiosity.

00:05:17.142 --> 00:05:20.052
And that helps us understand
why small children are

00:05:20.052 --> 00:05:21.502
curious about everything.

00:05:21.502 --> 00:05:22.872
They don't know anything.

00:05:22.872 --> 00:05:27.872
So they are well positioned
to be sponges and be

00:05:29.022 --> 00:05:31.352
curious about everything
because the world is

00:05:31.352 --> 00:05:34.062
offering all this information
and they don't know

00:05:34.062 --> 00:05:35.312
anything about the world.

00:05:36.832 --> 00:05:38.298
- That's great.

00:05:38.298 --> 00:05:40.382
So, as you said, you're
a cognitive psychologist

00:05:40.382 --> 00:05:42.394
and a professor of psychology.

00:05:42.394 --> 00:05:46.602
What was your path to getting
to that place in your career.

00:05:46.602 --> 00:05:50.212
How did you come to be a
cognitive psychologist?

00:05:50.212 --> 00:05:54.612
- I came to be a, it was
much a matter of curiosity.

00:05:54.612 --> 00:05:58.162
Both of my parents were
actually psychologists,

00:05:58.162 --> 00:06:00.052
neither one of them a
cognitive psychologist.

00:06:00.052 --> 00:06:03.952
But they both had degrees
in experimental psychology.

00:06:03.952 --> 00:06:06.112
My dad went off and
became a psychometrician,

00:06:06.112 --> 00:06:08.843
my mother later became a counselor.

00:06:08.843 --> 00:06:12.382
And my uncle was also an
experimental psychologist.

00:06:12.382 --> 00:06:16.362
So I was really determined
not to do anything

00:06:16.362 --> 00:06:18.274
in psychology at all.

00:06:18.274 --> 00:06:21.217
But then in college I thought
gosh, I guess I need to take

00:06:21.217 --> 00:06:24.567
like intro to psychology
or something so that

00:06:24.567 --> 00:06:27.342
I can keep up at Thanksgiving dinner.

00:06:27.342 --> 00:06:31.272
So I did and I found myself
absolutely fascinated.

00:06:31.272 --> 00:06:35.062
And then I started taking
a broader array of courses

00:06:35.062 --> 00:06:38.133
and cognitive psychology just really,

00:06:38.133 --> 00:06:42.582
I want to say, caught my
fancy, which is a weird

00:06:42.582 --> 00:06:45.632
turn of phrase, but that
pretty well describes it.

00:06:45.632 --> 00:06:47.052
I just couldn't get enough of it,

00:06:47.052 --> 00:06:50.272
and so decided to go
to graduate school and

00:06:50.272 --> 00:06:51.815
just keep doing what I loved.

00:06:53.802 --> 00:06:54.635
- That's great.

00:06:54.635 --> 00:06:57.622
And I love the, I don't want
anything to do with what

00:06:57.622 --> 00:06:59.472
my parents did, but oh that's actually

00:06:59.472 --> 00:07:00.672
kind of interesting too.

00:07:02.456 --> 00:07:04.622
Yeah, absolutely.

00:07:04.622 --> 00:07:08.492
So thinking generally across
your education experience

00:07:08.492 --> 00:07:10.982
were there any particularly
teachers who influenced

00:07:10.982 --> 00:07:15.175
you or that you found really
helped you on your path?

00:07:16.772 --> 00:07:17.605
- Oh my gosh.

00:07:17.605 --> 00:07:20.002
I had so many amazing teachers.

00:07:20.002 --> 00:07:23.952
I mentioned that I was
really captivated by

00:07:23.952 --> 00:07:26.942
cognitive psychology in college.

00:07:26.942 --> 00:07:30.362
And Ruth Day was the professor
who taught that course.

00:07:30.362 --> 00:07:32.142
And I took the psychology
of language course

00:07:32.142 --> 00:07:33.292
afterwards with her.

00:07:33.292 --> 00:07:35.112
I did a senior thesis with her.

00:07:35.112 --> 00:07:38.032
So she was certainly extremely influential

00:07:38.032 --> 00:07:40.422
in developing my love of psychology.

00:07:40.422 --> 00:07:44.172
I also have to single out
my fifth grade teacher,

00:07:44.172 --> 00:07:47.262
Richard Leewer, who was just a really

00:07:47.262 --> 00:07:52.262
really inspiring teacher and
really made me come to school

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:54.702
excited to learn every day

00:07:54.702 --> 00:07:59.452
and also really managed
to help me see that school

00:07:59.452 --> 00:08:01.382
was something I was doing for myself.

00:08:01.382 --> 00:08:05.152
I think up until that point,
I kind of went to school just

00:08:05.152 --> 00:08:07.742
because like you're a kid
and that's what you do.

00:08:07.742 --> 00:08:10.712
And like, to the extent
that I had any skin

00:08:10.712 --> 00:08:11.972
in the game, so to speak

00:08:11.972 --> 00:08:14.662
it was like I was doing this
to make my parents happy.

00:08:14.662 --> 00:08:17.752
Like if, if I didn't go, my
parents would be mad at me.

00:08:17.752 --> 00:08:21.012
And Mr. Leewer actually really made me see

00:08:21.012 --> 00:08:24.232
that learning was something that was fun

00:08:24.232 --> 00:08:26.282
and something that I
appreciated and something

00:08:26.282 --> 00:08:28.605
I benefited from.

00:08:28.605 --> 00:08:30.602
So, you know, I couldn't tell you exactly

00:08:30.602 --> 00:08:33.712
what content I learned
in fifth grade from him

00:08:33.712 --> 00:08:37.222
but I will never forget
that sort of attitude

00:08:37.222 --> 00:08:39.825
towards school and that
attitude towards learning.

00:08:41.782 --> 00:08:43.072
- That's great.

00:08:43.072 --> 00:08:46.012
Actually, when people ask
me about teachers, myself

00:08:46.012 --> 00:08:49.002
and my fifth grade teacher
was also very influential

00:08:49.002 --> 00:08:49.835
in what we did.

00:08:49.835 --> 00:08:50.902
So I wonder too

00:08:51.755 --> 00:08:54.802
if there's something about that
age, that that gets people.

00:08:54.802 --> 00:08:59.010
So do you, we were talking about how

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:03.302
that kind of inspiration,
but also building

00:09:03.302 --> 00:09:07.195
on what you learned and what
you know and where those are.

00:09:08.124 --> 00:09:09.302
So there's a lot of

00:09:09.302 --> 00:09:14.275
we talk about in motivation,
finding meaning for learning.

00:09:14.275 --> 00:09:16.722
And one of the things
that can help motivate

00:09:16.722 --> 00:09:18.712
students is finding meaning
in what they're doing.

00:09:18.712 --> 00:09:20.662
And I know lots of
teachers get the question

00:09:20.662 --> 00:09:22.202
of what am I going to use this?

00:09:22.202 --> 00:09:24.302
Why do I need this?

00:09:24.302 --> 00:09:27.180
How should we help build meaning?

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:28.013
And what do we do when it seems

00:09:28.013 --> 00:09:29.442
like the things that we need to learn

00:09:29.442 --> 00:09:30.842
don't have a lot of meaning?

00:09:32.142 --> 00:09:35.752
- Yeah, I mean, I comment
on this a little bit

00:09:35.752 --> 00:09:39.200
in "Why Don't Students Like
School" and in a way it's a

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:43.722
stance that concerns me a little bit.

00:09:43.722 --> 00:09:45.212
I don't feel

00:09:45.212 --> 00:09:50.022
like will students find
this meaningful now is

00:09:50.022 --> 00:09:54.852
a very good litmus test to
which we should hold content.

00:09:54.852 --> 00:09:57.052
And the reason for that
is I think there's a lot

00:09:57.052 --> 00:10:01.392
of things that students
will enjoy knowing,

00:10:01.392 --> 00:10:05.572
will find beautiful, will
maybe even find important

00:10:05.572 --> 00:10:09.592
that they don't find
meaningful in their life

00:10:09.592 --> 00:10:10.902
as they would describe it.

00:10:10.902 --> 00:10:13.452
In other words, if you try
and tempt them with, look

00:10:13.452 --> 00:10:17.012
this is something that is
important for you to know

00:10:17.012 --> 00:10:18.342
and you're going to find it interesting

00:10:18.342 --> 00:10:19.992
and important in your daily life.

00:10:19.992 --> 00:10:20.825
I think, first of all

00:10:20.825 --> 00:10:22.582
I think a lot of times that's going to be

00:10:22.582 --> 00:10:26.852
feel really strained for
us because a lot of what we

00:10:26.852 --> 00:10:30.972
as adults kind of in
our more mature judgment

00:10:30.972 --> 00:10:35.445
think this is important for a
fifth grader to learn about.

00:10:36.382 --> 00:10:39.642
It's not immediately obvious
to the fifth grader that yes

00:10:39.642 --> 00:10:42.582
that's going to be meaningful to me.

00:10:42.582 --> 00:10:45.802
But in contrast, we can, we
were talking about curiosity.

00:10:45.802 --> 00:10:48.702
You can make people curious
about things to say like, look

00:10:48.702 --> 00:10:52.202
here's this puzzle, here's
this odd, here's this fact

00:10:52.202 --> 00:10:55.242
in mathematics, like how,
why is it work that way?

00:10:55.242 --> 00:10:56.802
Or here's this fact in science?

00:10:56.802 --> 00:10:58.435
Why, what is that all about?

00:10:58.435 --> 00:11:00.202
I think that's a more

00:11:00.202 --> 00:11:02.332
when we're talking about motivation, yes.

00:11:02.332 --> 00:11:05.362
Things seeming personally
meaningful, personally relevant

00:11:05.362 --> 00:11:07.632
to me that can be powerful

00:11:07.632 --> 00:11:11.772
but I think that will
greatly narrow the scope

00:11:11.772 --> 00:11:15.522
of what it is we want
students to know about.

00:11:15.522 --> 00:11:18.672
Also worry a little bit candidly, that it

00:11:18.672 --> 00:11:20.792
it sends an implicit message

00:11:20.792 --> 00:11:23.872
that the students are kind
of in the driver's seat.

00:11:23.872 --> 00:11:27.622
In other words, if I'm continually
trying to show students

00:11:27.622 --> 00:11:30.022
this is meaningful to
you, this is meaningful.

00:11:30.022 --> 00:11:33.316
That also sort of implies the contra,

00:11:33.316 --> 00:11:35.172
the counterpoint that, well

00:11:35.172 --> 00:11:37.062
if I don't think it's meaningful

00:11:37.062 --> 00:11:39.822
then that maybe means I
shouldn't be learning it.

00:11:39.822 --> 00:11:41.432
And I, I don't think

00:11:41.432 --> 00:11:44.752
that fifth graders
should have that control.

00:11:44.752 --> 00:11:47.442
I think that teachers are
the ones who should be

00:11:47.442 --> 00:11:49.522
deciding this is what's important

00:11:49.522 --> 00:11:50.672
for you to learn right now.

00:11:50.672 --> 00:11:54.285
And it's incumbent on me to
make you intrigued about it.

00:11:56.652 --> 00:11:59.452
- Interesting, so I
just, as you were talking

00:11:59.452 --> 00:12:02.252
had this thought, as
you said that about them

00:12:02.252 --> 00:12:03.972
that deciding it, also triggered

00:12:03.972 --> 00:12:05.712
in me some of the conversations

00:12:05.712 --> 00:12:10.712
around diversity and
inclusion and that just

00:12:10.942 --> 00:12:15.942
because it's not relevant to
me, let's say the, you know

00:12:16.588 --> 00:12:19.582
upper middle-class white fifth grader

00:12:19.582 --> 00:12:22.202
in the suburban school, doesn't mean

00:12:22.202 --> 00:12:24.062
that it's not something
that I should know to

00:12:24.062 --> 00:12:25.912
be able to understand the experiences

00:12:25.912 --> 00:12:29.882
of other people too, and where that is.

00:12:29.882 --> 00:12:32.166
So I wonder if there's a link there.

00:12:32.166 --> 00:12:32.999
- I agree.

00:12:32.999 --> 00:12:36.892
I mean, I think that's
especially point, not poignant,

00:12:36.892 --> 00:12:40.712
but it's especially important
to consider in the realm

00:12:40.712 --> 00:12:43.912
of human experience to so,
so to sort of say, like

00:12:43.912 --> 00:12:46.702
what do I have to learn about this story

00:12:46.702 --> 00:12:49.182
that's about somebody
who's totally unlike me?

00:12:49.182 --> 00:12:51.512
Like, no, that's kind of the point is

00:12:51.512 --> 00:12:54.012
first of all, it's interesting

00:12:54.012 --> 00:12:56.272
and important to learn
about the perspectives

00:12:56.272 --> 00:12:59.042
of people from other cultures,
people who are not like you.

00:12:59.042 --> 00:13:00.152
And second of all,

00:13:00.152 --> 00:13:02.222
you're gonna see some
similarities that maybe

00:13:02.222 --> 00:13:03.242
you didn't expect,

00:13:03.242 --> 00:13:06.012
that there is universality
in the human experience.

00:13:06.012 --> 00:13:08.062
And how are you going to know that

00:13:08.062 --> 00:13:10.662
unless you read about some people

00:13:10.662 --> 00:13:11.765
who are different than you are?

00:13:12.662 --> 00:13:15.642
- Yeah, absolutely fascinating.

00:13:15.642 --> 00:13:17.252
I do want to get a little bit

00:13:17.252 --> 00:13:22.252
to talking about in the
realm of technology.

00:13:22.822 --> 00:13:26.912
So in COVID and we've, you know

00:13:26.912 --> 00:13:29.882
even more started relying on
digital learning environments.

00:13:29.882 --> 00:13:34.022
And one of the things that
we hear sometimes is are all

00:13:34.022 --> 00:13:36.372
of these phones and screens changing

00:13:36.372 --> 00:13:38.222
how people learn are
they getting different

00:13:38.222 --> 00:13:39.132
dopamine hits?

00:13:39.132 --> 00:13:41.552
Are our attention spans changing?

00:13:41.552 --> 00:13:43.812
What are your thoughts on that?

00:13:43.812 --> 00:13:45.862
- Well, there's a lot of pieces, you know

00:13:47.356 --> 00:13:48.189
just in your question,
right, there was really

00:13:48.189 --> 00:13:51.852
like three different ones in
there about whether, you know

00:13:51.852 --> 00:13:53.662
are screens changing our brains?

00:13:53.662 --> 00:13:54.622
Do we learn?

00:13:54.622 --> 00:13:55.952
Let's take those two questions.

00:13:55.952 --> 00:13:57.132
Are screens changing our brains?

00:13:57.132 --> 00:13:59.495
And then do we learn
differently from screens?

00:14:00.392 --> 00:14:03.852
The idea that screens are
changing our brains, I've argued

00:14:03.852 --> 00:14:05.862
that's very improbable.

00:14:05.862 --> 00:14:07.112
And the reason I argue that,

00:14:07.112 --> 00:14:10.632
the main way that people have suspected

00:14:10.632 --> 00:14:12.382
that screens are changing our brains is

00:14:12.382 --> 00:14:16.392
that they are reducing
attention span and/or

00:14:16.392 --> 00:14:18.955
reducing working memory span.

00:14:18.955 --> 00:14:22.052
First of all, we have data
that that's not happening.

00:14:22.052 --> 00:14:23.892
So there are certain tests

00:14:23.892 --> 00:14:26.362
of working memory that
had been administered

00:14:26.362 --> 00:14:28.262
for close to a hundred years.

00:14:28.262 --> 00:14:32.272
And so we actually have data
from limited populations, not

00:14:32.272 --> 00:14:34.452
you know, worldwide or anything

00:14:34.452 --> 00:14:36.972
but we certainly have American samples.

00:14:36.972 --> 00:14:39.142
And we know that working memory span just

00:14:39.142 --> 00:14:42.662
hasn't changed with
the digital revolution.

00:14:42.662 --> 00:14:43.792
So actually data on that.

00:14:43.792 --> 00:14:46.628
And then also from a
theoretical perspective

00:14:46.628 --> 00:14:50.582
you just wouldn't expect,
the brain is plastic.

00:14:50.582 --> 00:14:52.802
This is frequently what you
hear, the brain is plastic.

00:14:52.802 --> 00:14:55.872
And so how could it not
change in response to the

00:14:55.872 --> 00:14:57.847
all this experience with screens?

00:14:57.847 --> 00:15:00.552
And what I would say to
that is the brain is plastic

00:15:00.552 --> 00:15:02.192
but it can't be that plastic.

00:15:02.192 --> 00:15:04.862
So take working memory in particular.

00:15:04.862 --> 00:15:07.962
There are so many cognitive
processes that depend

00:15:07.962 --> 00:15:12.072
on working memory, reading,
problem solving, mathematics,

00:15:12.072 --> 00:15:14.402
all the things we care
about in school basically,

00:15:14.402 --> 00:15:16.652
really depend on working memory capacity.

00:15:16.652 --> 00:15:19.672
So if working memory
capacity were really plastic

00:15:19.672 --> 00:15:21.452
and really change with the environment

00:15:21.452 --> 00:15:25.222
and it shrunk the impact would cascade

00:15:25.222 --> 00:15:27.052
throughout the cognitive system.

00:15:27.052 --> 00:15:30.722
And basically everybody
would be really stupid

00:15:30.722 --> 00:15:34.542
if working memory capacity,
shrunk significantly.

00:15:34.542 --> 00:15:36.642
Now in terms of whether or
not we learn differently

00:15:36.642 --> 00:15:39.535
from screens, it's
pretty clear that we do.

00:15:39.535 --> 00:15:44.022
And just think about Zoom fatigue.

00:15:44.022 --> 00:15:45.802
That's enough to start to with, I mean

00:15:45.802 --> 00:15:48.842
this is what everybody is
found during the pandemic.

00:15:48.842 --> 00:15:53.532
The signal that you're getting
from a screen is not as rich

00:15:53.532 --> 00:15:56.792
as the signal that you get
from a live human being.

00:15:56.792 --> 00:15:58.892
And so one of the ways you're dealing

00:15:58.892 --> 00:16:03.262
with that is your expending
more cognitive effort

00:16:03.262 --> 00:16:05.602
trying to make up for that signal.

00:16:05.602 --> 00:16:08.183
The, you know, you don't, for one thing

00:16:08.183 --> 00:16:09.112
you frequently don't have gestures.

00:16:09.112 --> 00:16:11.277
You can occasionally see,
I sort of self-consciously

00:16:11.277 --> 00:16:13.972
I'm trying to keep my hands up here.

00:16:13.972 --> 00:16:17.702
So you can getting a little
bit more signal from that.

00:16:17.702 --> 00:16:18.762
Frequently though, you know,

00:16:18.762 --> 00:16:20.462
you're looking at a talking head,

00:16:20.462 --> 00:16:22.635
you're not getting any gestures.

00:16:22.635 --> 00:16:26.412
The two dimensionality
obviously makes a difference.

00:16:26.412 --> 00:16:27.682
The, you get a lag.

00:16:27.682 --> 00:16:29.992
There's all these problems with screens.

00:16:29.992 --> 00:16:33.162
And so what the viewer is doing is they're

00:16:33.162 --> 00:16:34.312
expending more cognitive energy

00:16:34.312 --> 00:16:38.174
trying to extract everything
they can from that signal.

00:16:38.174 --> 00:16:39.962
So learning from screens

00:16:39.962 --> 00:16:42.972
in there when, little kids
are not very good at this.

00:16:42.972 --> 00:16:46.062
Little kids really have
problems with screens.

00:16:46.062 --> 00:16:48.822
And if you Google the term video deficit

00:16:48.822 --> 00:16:52.553
you can find some of this, some
of this research literature.

00:16:52.553 --> 00:16:55.652
So certainly you can learn from screens

00:16:55.652 --> 00:16:57.407
but it's not the same experience.

00:16:57.407 --> 00:17:01.352
And we've made enormous
strides in the last 10

00:17:01.352 --> 00:17:04.668
or 15 years in figuring out
how to do this effectively

00:17:04.668 --> 00:17:08.295
but there are still issues
that we're trying to overcome.

00:17:10.602 --> 00:17:11.512
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:17:11.512 --> 00:17:15.402
So do you have recommendations
about, you know,

00:17:15.402 --> 00:17:17.322
screen time and the amounts

00:17:17.322 --> 00:17:20.434
of how much should parents
or teachers be thinking

00:17:20.434 --> 00:17:23.562
about the amount of time
students are spending on screens?

00:17:23.562 --> 00:17:26.452
- I mean like most people I'm, you know

00:17:26.452 --> 00:17:27.692
I'm very quick to say,

00:17:27.692 --> 00:17:30.565
well it depends on what
they're doing on their screens.

00:17:31.471 --> 00:17:32.712
But the truth is like

00:17:32.712 --> 00:17:37.712
if your child is unsupervised,
when they're, you know

00:17:38.242 --> 00:17:40.592
have a laptop or have an iPad or something

00:17:40.592 --> 00:17:42.532
you kind of know what they're doing.

00:17:42.532 --> 00:17:45.947
It's not, they're not looking
at Shakespeare Concordances.

00:17:45.947 --> 00:17:48.492
There are data on this that they're

00:17:48.492 --> 00:17:50.902
they're mostly consuming video content.

00:17:50.902 --> 00:17:52.482
They're texting their friends.

00:17:52.482 --> 00:17:54.417
I mean, the way I put it in

00:17:54.417 --> 00:17:55.942
"Why Students Don't Like School"

00:17:55.942 --> 00:17:57.992
is they're basically doing the stuff

00:17:57.992 --> 00:18:00.392
that kids have always liked to do.

00:18:00.392 --> 00:18:03.372
And they're just doing it with technology.

00:18:03.372 --> 00:18:05.622
Now, when I was left to my own devices

00:18:05.622 --> 00:18:08.702
when I was nine years old,
when I was 15 years old

00:18:08.702 --> 00:18:12.432
I was mostly not doing
the most enriching things.

00:18:12.432 --> 00:18:16.252
And so it's really less
about like, you know,

00:18:16.252 --> 00:18:17.972
what do you want your kids to be doing?

00:18:17.972 --> 00:18:20.132
Is it really the screen per se?

00:18:20.132 --> 00:18:24.428
Or is it that, gosh, I wish
they were reading, you know

00:18:24.428 --> 00:18:25.261
reading more books,

00:18:25.261 --> 00:18:26.792
or I wish they were
reading more non-fiction,

00:18:26.792 --> 00:18:30.082
or I wish they were working
on their social skills more,

00:18:30.082 --> 00:18:33.304
whatever it is, whatever
it is you want them to do

00:18:33.304 --> 00:18:35.232
they can probably do it on a screen.

00:18:35.232 --> 00:18:37.493
They could probably do it off a screen.

00:18:37.493 --> 00:18:39.392
So you need to figure

00:18:39.392 --> 00:18:43.022
out how you're going to
encourage your child to engage

00:18:43.022 --> 00:18:45.032
in more of the activities they want.

00:18:45.032 --> 00:18:46.422
I think the thing that really bugs people

00:18:46.422 --> 00:18:48.712
about screen time is spend times mostly

00:18:48.712 --> 00:18:50.502
on supervised and yeah, most

00:18:50.502 --> 00:18:52.939
of what kids do when they're
unsupervised on screens is

00:18:52.939 --> 00:18:55.842
you know, in the long
run, not super enriching.

00:18:55.842 --> 00:18:58.365
It's kind of fun for them,
but not very enriching.

00:19:00.202 --> 00:19:03.272
I have to say (audio distorting)

00:19:03.272 --> 00:19:06.107
this is especially
acute with the pandemic.

00:19:06.107 --> 00:19:08.722
Like most parents, we had
lots of rules in my house

00:19:08.722 --> 00:19:11.542
and then the pandemic
struck and my wife and I

00:19:11.542 --> 00:19:13.892
are like, just play Among Us all you want.

00:19:13.892 --> 00:19:16.782
Whatever it is, this has
just been a nightmare

00:19:16.782 --> 00:19:18.912
for everybody, like, you
know, I can't blame you.

00:19:18.912 --> 00:19:21.021
Just do whatever you want to do.

00:19:21.021 --> 00:19:25.104
We're trying to (cross talking).

00:19:26.859 --> 00:19:28.892
- Absolutely, absolutely.

00:19:28.892 --> 00:19:30.232
So we talked about screens.

00:19:30.232 --> 00:19:32.722
There's actually a comment from YouTube,

00:19:32.722 --> 00:19:36.742
Bencat Parkenen who says that more audio

00:19:36.742 --> 00:19:37.962
learning should be implemented.

00:19:37.962 --> 00:19:40.002
I never use audio learning
in my high school,

00:19:40.002 --> 00:19:42.212
but it's so much powerful now.

00:19:42.212 --> 00:19:45.362
And so I wonder if do
you have thoughts about

00:19:45.362 --> 00:19:46.832
how learning is impacted

00:19:46.832 --> 00:19:49.615
by different communication
methods or channels?

00:19:50.952 --> 00:19:55.172
- Yeah, I mean that's been
pretty carefully researched

00:19:55.172 --> 00:19:57.622
when it comes to reading.

00:19:57.622 --> 00:20:01.782
So the comparison being
reading text versus listening

00:20:01.782 --> 00:20:04.122
to an audiobook.

00:20:04.122 --> 00:20:07.512
I don't know that there's
been a lot of research on

00:20:07.512 --> 00:20:09.889
audio learning more broadly.

00:20:09.889 --> 00:20:13.411
There's a whole lot of, so I'll comment on

00:20:13.411 --> 00:20:17.842
reading from print versus an audio book.

00:20:17.842 --> 00:20:21.232
There's a whole lot of overlap
in the mental machinery

00:20:21.232 --> 00:20:24.562
that comprehends printed text versus

00:20:24.562 --> 00:20:27.917
listening the to someone
read the same text.

00:20:27.917 --> 00:20:31.772
This is assuming that
you are a very smooth

00:20:31.772 --> 00:20:34.822
fluent decoder when you are reading print.

00:20:34.822 --> 00:20:36.912
So in other words, going
from print on the page

00:20:36.912 --> 00:20:39.582
to words in the mind
is no problem for you.

00:20:39.582 --> 00:20:41.982
You're quite fassel with that.

00:20:41.982 --> 00:20:44.722
If you are there's a whole
lot of overlap between

00:20:44.722 --> 00:20:46.252
the two processes.

00:20:46.252 --> 00:20:49.672
Because the processes
that you use to comprehend

00:20:49.672 --> 00:20:52.002
written text is basically the same process

00:20:52.002 --> 00:20:53.692
you use for oral language.

00:20:53.692 --> 00:20:57.082
There's some differences
around the margins,

00:20:57.082 --> 00:20:57.952
which are not trivial.

00:20:57.952 --> 00:20:59.792
One that's pretty obvious
is that when you're

00:20:59.792 --> 00:21:02.032
listening to a book
someone's reading it to you.

00:21:02.032 --> 00:21:06.126
And so you get what scientists
call prosodic information.

00:21:06.126 --> 00:21:11.126
Prosidy is sort of
music of speech and it's

00:21:13.282 --> 00:21:17.472
what tells you the difference
between what a great party

00:21:17.472 --> 00:21:19.322
and what a great party, right.

00:21:19.322 --> 00:21:23.072
So sarcasm is all a matter of prosity.

00:21:23.072 --> 00:21:26.432
And so in that sense listening
to a text is a little bit

00:21:26.432 --> 00:21:29.442
easier because the, whoever
is doing the reading

00:21:29.442 --> 00:21:32.682
is giving you that prosodic information.

00:21:32.682 --> 00:21:35.262
On the other hand when
you're reading print

00:21:35.262 --> 00:21:38.349
it's very easy to move
backwards in a text.

00:21:38.349 --> 00:21:41.582
You control the pace you can
read as quickly and slowly

00:21:41.582 --> 00:21:43.982
as you want and it's real
easy to go backwards.

00:21:43.982 --> 00:21:47.412
And if you look at eye
movements while people

00:21:47.412 --> 00:21:49.312
are reading something like 10,

00:21:49.312 --> 00:21:52.445
15% of eye movements are
actually regressions where you're

00:21:52.445 --> 00:21:53.482
going back.

00:21:53.482 --> 00:21:55.942
You had a little bit of trouble unraveling

00:21:55.942 --> 00:21:58.872
the syntax of a sentence
maybe so you go back to

00:21:58.872 --> 00:22:00.215
pick up a few words.

00:22:00.215 --> 00:22:03.702
In principle you can go
back when you're listening

00:22:03.702 --> 00:22:04.712
to an audiobook.

00:22:04.712 --> 00:22:07.992
But in practice it's kind
of awkward and most people

00:22:07.992 --> 00:22:08.862
don't really do it.

00:22:08.862 --> 00:22:13.862
And of course you're not in
terrific control of the pace.

00:22:14.206 --> 00:22:18.812
So that makes audiobooks
slightly more difficult.

00:22:18.812 --> 00:22:22.252
So again, lots and lots
of overlap, but the,

00:22:22.252 --> 00:22:24.595
some differences at the margins.

00:22:26.752 --> 00:22:28.502
- Got it. Thank you.

00:22:28.502 --> 00:22:33.292
We've got a few more questions
coming in on Facebook.

00:22:33.292 --> 00:22:36.582
So, one of the things we keep hearing

00:22:36.582 --> 00:22:39.432
and Shelly Acosta Thompson asks a question

00:22:39.432 --> 00:22:41.142
related to that now is

00:22:41.142 --> 00:22:43.712
how do you keep teens motivated,

00:22:43.712 --> 00:22:48.386
especially this year, but
over time it seems to be

00:22:48.386 --> 00:22:52.761
an ongoing question of how
we keep them interested

00:22:52.761 --> 00:22:53.982
in wanting to learn.

00:22:53.982 --> 00:22:56.525
- Yeah, how we keep students
interested in school.

00:22:57.882 --> 00:22:59.962
- Particularly teens, but yes.

00:22:59.962 --> 00:23:01.312
- Particularly teens, yeah,

00:23:02.338 --> 00:23:04.297
and I don't know whether the
question is really like from

00:23:04.297 --> 00:23:07.542
a parental perspective versus
from a teacher's perspective.

00:23:07.542 --> 00:23:09.952
- It is, she has a 14 year old.

00:23:09.952 --> 00:23:13.502
- So it's from a parental perspective.

00:23:13.502 --> 00:23:17.322
Yeah, I mean it gets difficult
because lots of other things

00:23:17.322 --> 00:23:20.442
crowd around in on their time.

00:23:20.442 --> 00:23:23.962
And teens in particular
are become extraordinarily

00:23:23.962 --> 00:23:26.695
interested in their social lives.

00:23:26.695 --> 00:23:30.952
BJ Casey is a psychologist
at Yale University,

00:23:30.952 --> 00:23:33.827
has a very good take on this I think.

00:23:33.827 --> 00:23:36.752
She points out that this
is practice for them,

00:23:36.752 --> 00:23:40.102
this is preparation for
becoming independent

00:23:40.102 --> 00:23:41.372
of their parents.

00:23:41.372 --> 00:23:45.312
So they're becoming extremely
attuned to their peers

00:23:45.312 --> 00:23:48.392
because they're now
learning how to navigate

00:23:48.392 --> 00:23:51.972
this world where other
people are going to be

00:23:51.972 --> 00:23:55.872
important as they move out of their home.

00:23:55.872 --> 00:24:00.062
So, I that kind of
competition, trig just does not

00:24:00.062 --> 00:24:03.012
seem to hold a whole lot of appeal.

00:24:03.012 --> 00:24:08.012
I think one of the things
that is important to do,

00:24:08.472 --> 00:24:11.576
and it's certainly not gonna
be a full blown solution,

00:24:11.576 --> 00:24:14.822
is to emphasis that this is a family value

00:24:14.822 --> 00:24:17.382
and I hope that this is
something that has been

00:24:17.382 --> 00:24:19.692
a family value all along.

00:24:19.692 --> 00:24:21.832
And so sort of acknowledge and validate

00:24:21.832 --> 00:24:23.532
like I get it that there are other things

00:24:23.532 --> 00:24:25.212
you're interested in, I also get it.

00:24:25.212 --> 00:24:28.332
Mr. Willingham, your history
teacher is pretty boring.

00:24:28.332 --> 00:24:32.117
That's legit. I'm not
saying that he's not.

00:24:32.117 --> 00:24:35.872
At the same time some
things have not changed.

00:24:35.872 --> 00:24:38.108
And one of the things
that hasn't changed is

00:24:38.108 --> 00:24:40.942
in this family we take school seriously.

00:24:40.942 --> 00:24:42.662
We show up on time.

00:24:42.662 --> 00:24:47.322
We are, talk politely to our teachers,

00:24:47.322 --> 00:24:50.932
all the sorts of things that
you've been doing all along

00:24:50.932 --> 00:24:52.552
are important and
continue to be important.

00:24:52.552 --> 00:24:54.182
And one of the things that continues

00:24:54.182 --> 00:24:57.862
to be important is trying
your best in all your classes

00:24:57.862 --> 00:25:00.772
and making sure that
you make time for that.

00:25:00.772 --> 00:25:04.822
So, in general I'm very
much a fan of trying to

00:25:04.822 --> 00:25:08.702
start with positivity and trying to start

00:25:08.702 --> 00:25:12.482
with sort of appealing to the best

00:25:12.482 --> 00:25:15.242
in sort of a teen's sense of self,

00:25:15.242 --> 00:25:17.605
like start saying, this is who you are.

00:25:17.605 --> 00:25:20.692
This is the best version
of you that you want to be.

00:25:20.692 --> 00:25:23.552
You want to be the kind of, or you are the

00:25:23.552 --> 00:25:25.712
kind of person who treats school this way.

00:25:25.712 --> 00:25:29.052
As opposed to the punitive
approach, like well look,

00:25:29.052 --> 00:25:30.512
if you're not gonna do you work then I'm

00:25:30.512 --> 00:25:32.021
gonna take your iPad away.

00:25:32.021 --> 00:25:35.332
Because that's obviously not appealing

00:25:35.332 --> 00:25:37.752
to the best in the student.

00:25:37.752 --> 00:25:40.812
And I also find that tit
ends up being a little bit

00:25:40.812 --> 00:25:42.452
of a game of cat and mouse.

00:25:42.452 --> 00:25:44.812
It's sort of like, well,
you're not doing what I want

00:25:44.812 --> 00:25:47.842
you to do therefore I'm
gonna do this to you.

00:25:47.842 --> 00:25:50.672
And that to me sort of sets up a bargain

00:25:50.672 --> 00:25:51.842
in the child's mind.

00:25:51.842 --> 00:25:55.662
Like, okay, so if I can
still get away with not doing

00:25:55.662 --> 00:25:57.862
what you want me to do and retain my iPad

00:25:57.862 --> 00:25:59.482
then I'm kind of winning.

00:25:59.482 --> 00:26:02.722
Whereas if I'm appealing
to their sense of self

00:26:02.722 --> 00:26:05.855
then they themselves kind of know whether

00:26:05.855 --> 00:26:08.008
or not they're living up to that.

00:26:08.008 --> 00:26:12.295
So, if I can do it that's
gonna be my starting place.

00:26:14.662 --> 00:26:16.332
- Got it. Thank you.

00:26:16.332 --> 00:26:19.212
And I know that's a whole
how to motivate teens

00:26:19.212 --> 00:26:21.642
could be a whole series of books.

00:26:21.642 --> 00:26:24.022
- Oh god, god help us all.

00:26:24.022 --> 00:26:27.950
I mean that's, as the father
of two teens right now

00:26:27.950 --> 00:26:29.402
this is a very difficult problem.

00:26:29.402 --> 00:26:30.925
I feel your pain for sure.

00:26:32.022 --> 00:26:33.952
- Definitely, definitely.

00:26:33.952 --> 00:26:37.702
So, in thinking about motivation,

00:26:37.702 --> 00:26:40.572
you know we talked
earlier about curiosity.

00:26:40.572 --> 00:26:43.262
I wonder if there's something too with,

00:26:43.262 --> 00:26:46.459
that really thinking about
motivation, curiosity

00:26:46.459 --> 00:26:50.442
and I know you talked
recently about creativity too

00:26:50.442 --> 00:26:53.692
and if there's appealing
to students creativity

00:26:53.692 --> 00:26:57.552
or opportunities there
to encourage students

00:26:57.552 --> 00:26:59.985
into being interested in learning.

00:27:00.972 --> 00:27:03.152
- I think absolutely.

00:27:03.152 --> 00:27:06.732
And this is something that
I think most parents want

00:27:06.732 --> 00:27:10.102
to do this and I think
most parents are doing it

00:27:10.102 --> 00:27:13.842
as much as they can, but
encouraging creative outlets

00:27:13.842 --> 00:27:16.262
that are not necessarily part of school

00:27:16.262 --> 00:27:19.290
I think is a wonderful opportunity to keep

00:27:19.290 --> 00:27:22.724
kids engaged with
learning, especially kids

00:27:22.724 --> 00:27:27.172
who are struggling in a lot
of their school subjects.

00:27:27.172 --> 00:27:31.592
That feeling that because
what you're really worried

00:27:31.592 --> 00:27:34.712
about and trying to
guard against is a sense

00:27:34.712 --> 00:27:39.255
of self that doesn't
include learning at all.

00:27:39.255 --> 00:27:42.205
It's sort of like, well,
school is not a place where

00:27:42.205 --> 00:27:43.432
I succeed.

00:27:43.432 --> 00:27:46.144
School is not a place where
I feel good about myself

00:27:46.144 --> 00:27:49.832
and so that sort of extends to any type of

00:27:49.832 --> 00:27:51.812
learning that might happen, period, right.

00:27:51.812 --> 00:27:53.492
Because school is where you learn.

00:27:53.492 --> 00:27:57.082
In contrast to if a
student can feel like yeah,

00:27:57.082 --> 00:27:59.752
the stuff I do at school I
really struggle with that.

00:27:59.752 --> 00:28:01.358
That's really hard for me.

00:28:01.358 --> 00:28:05.722
I'm not sure I see that
in my future long term.

00:28:05.722 --> 00:28:09.002
But then there's this
other content that I feel

00:28:09.002 --> 00:28:10.312
really passionate about.

00:28:10.312 --> 00:28:14.262
I know I'm good at that and that entails

00:28:14.262 --> 00:28:17.078
learning and I'm good
at that type of learning

00:28:17.078 --> 00:28:20.572
that seems like a really
desirable way to go.

00:28:20.572 --> 00:28:22.402
And it doesn't, obviously
it doesn't just apply

00:28:22.402 --> 00:28:24.102
to students who are struggling in school.

00:28:24.102 --> 00:28:27.122
This is, you know, having
that passion, especially

00:28:27.122 --> 00:28:29.632
feeling like they can
go deep on something.

00:28:29.632 --> 00:28:31.552
One of the things I know is so frustrating

00:28:31.552 --> 00:28:35.122
for students is everything
is sort of a million

00:28:35.122 --> 00:28:36.922
miles wide and two inches deep.

00:28:36.922 --> 00:28:39.772
And so getting to the
point where they feel like

00:28:39.772 --> 00:28:41.912
they're really getting mastery.

00:28:41.912 --> 00:28:45.012
And they know more about
this than most adults do.

00:28:45.012 --> 00:28:48.432
That's like, that's
really exciting for them.

00:28:48.432 --> 00:28:51.695
And every kid deserves to feel that way.

00:28:53.462 --> 00:28:56.662
- Absolutely, that seems like a good note.

00:28:56.662 --> 00:28:58.652
The half hour has flown by.

00:28:58.652 --> 00:29:00.902
So we will wrap it up there.

00:29:00.902 --> 00:29:03.192
Thank you so much for your time today.

00:29:03.192 --> 00:29:05.122
Again folks, Dr. Dan Willingham.

00:29:05.122 --> 00:29:08.222
Check out the second edition of his book

00:29:08.222 --> 00:29:11.852
that's out now and the, we'll remind

00:29:11.852 --> 00:29:15.562
all of you as well that
tomorrow at the same

00:29:15.562 --> 00:29:18.442
time we have Ben Gomes who will be talking

00:29:18.442 --> 00:29:21.652
about new learning initiatives at Google.

00:29:21.652 --> 00:29:23.142
So you can join us then.

00:29:23.142 --> 00:29:26.932
Thanks again to Dr. Dan
Willingham for joining us.

00:29:26.932 --> 00:29:29.965
All of you have a good day
and we will see you next time.

00:29:35.855 --> 00:29:38.438
